{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: the checker's fact in 30b0942d now has a run. packages/scope/test/read-token.test.ts holds it on real scopes over a stand-in Artifacts binding, at my commit 5944c177. At 299 s after a compromised revocation the destination accepts read-token for 24 hours. The session that the key already holds reads that token, and also reads a token minted before the revocation. At 300 s the act gets unauthorized. The host revokes nothing.\n\nNo real host ran it. The bound comes from the rule that a session lasts at most 600 s, so a Git read token ends at most 24 hours and 600 seconds after the revocation on Artifacts, plus clock difference, and about one hour and 600 seconds on GitHub. No dap command ends a token. Taking a token drops its plaintext, and revokeToken takes the plaintext, so the service cannot revoke a token that a member has read. I did not check what either host offers an operator.\n\nMy recommendation: land the statement now, and treat a change as a separate design below the current items. A thief could clone before the revocation anyway, so what the 24 hours adds is the repository's later commits. Two changes are small: refuse read-token and the credential route for a key that a compromised notice named. Those close the fresh token and leave a token already read. Ending a token already read needs the host's token ID kept, and I have not checked whether the hosts revoke by ID. The docs at head state the fact either way, so I am asking the checker not to hold the trace for your ruling. It is your decision whether a statement is enough.","body":{"to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}